{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0141s2AXk7LGwLgx7mwbFLoS","type":"tool_result","content":"156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n166:## Scanners\n175:**The output schema is strict.** Each line is one JSON object with exactly these keys; blank lines are skipped. `fingerprint` (required, 1 to 512 bytes of valid UTF-8, no control characters), `title` (required, one line, at most 200 characters), `severity` (required: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low` or `info`), `group` (optional; empty means one issue per finding), `files` (optional repo-relative paths, clean, no `..`) and `body` (optional markdown, at most 64 KiB). An unknown key, a bad value, a duplicate fingerprint, a line over 1 MiB, a stream over 16 MiB or more than 10,000 findings is an invalid line, and **any invalid line fails the whole run**: nothing is filed, closed or changed, so a half-parsed run can never trigger autoclose. Titles, bodies and file names come from repository content, so the controller treats them as untrusted data: before they reach an issue body it escapes HTML comment delimiters, so scanner output cannot forge a marker.\n179:**The pre-filing guard cannot be turned off.** After every scan and before the diff, in dry-run too and even with zero findings, the controller scans the title, fingerprint, group, files and body of every finding with the builtin pattern scanner and the controller's known secret values, plus gitleaks when `redact.gitleaks.enabled`. A hit, a builtin-pass failure or an invalid line changes nothing: no issue is filed, joined, edited, ticked or closed, no finding row is written from the run's findings (reconcile and the dry-run ledger adoption, which read GitHub and the ledger, still run before the scan), and the run is recorded as `scan_failed`. When gitleaks is enabled but cannot run, nothing is filed, joined or edited, but autoclose of already-filed issues still proceeds (an issue is ticked only when it closes) unless `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing: true`; the run is still `scan_failed`. Logs name only the rule and the field of a hit (known values by env var name); a pass failure also logs its error. A shutdown during the guard records no run.\n186:2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included (`none` and `needs-human` issues count too, and are never stale-closed, so a non-autoclose scanner with those routes stops once its issues fill `max_open` until a human closes or promotes some): a scanner at its cap does not run with a zero filing budget, so it cannot autoclose its own fixed issues until a human or the stale closer frees a slot. A global cap across all scanners (`global_max_open`) stops a bad day from burying the queue. A skipped run is not queued: it counts as a run, and the next scheduled run checks again.\n188:4. **Diff.** A new, unsuppressed fingerprint is queued for filing. A suppressed fingerprint stays quiet unless its severity rises above the suppressed one. A fingerprint that returns after being fixed files a new issue that links the old one as a regression. An open issue whose fingerprints are all gone gets \"No longer detected at `<sha>`\" and is closed, but only for scanners marked `autoclose` (deterministic ones); other scanners never edit or close an issue for absence. A returning finding unticks its line.\n220:**The error-codes scanner** (`scanners/error-codes`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, pattern match over source, registry and docs files) reads a human-owned `codes.json` in its role directory; nothing ships one, because error-code conventions are specific to each repository. Keys: `pattern` (required, a regex of at most 512 characters, wrapped so `E1001` does not match inside `XE10011`; it must not match the empty string), `registry` (required, a list of path patterns), `entry` (optional: a registry line must also match it to define a code), `docs` (optional; unset turns the undocumented check off), `sources` (optional, default every text file) and `exclude`. A path pattern ending in `/` is a directory prefix from the root, one without `/` matches the base name, and any other is a glob on the whole path (`*` and `?` stop at `/`, `**` does not). Unknown keys, wrong types and lists over 100 entries are errors. Codes found in registry files (on `entry` lines) are R, codes anywhere in docs files are D, codes in every other text file are S. Findings: `error-codes:duplicated:<code>` (high, group `duplicate codes`: a code on two or more distinct registry lines), `error-codes:unregistered:<code>` (medium, group `unregistered codes`: in S, not in R) and `error-codes:undocumented:<code>` (low, group `undocumented codes`: in R, not in D, only when `docs` is set). Fingerprints use the code alone, so moving a use never churns an issue. `.git`, `.ard`, `vendor`, `node_modules`, `testdata`, symlinks, binary files, files of about 1 MiB or more and lines over 16 KiB are skipped. A finding carries only the code and repo-relative `path:line` locations, never line text, and codes outside `[A-Za-z0-9_.:/+=-]{1,128}` are dropped with a stderr count. Because the scanner is `autoclose`, it fails the run rather than see less: an invalid `codes.json`, a `registry` or `docs` that matches no file, a file in both, an unreadable file, a failing tool or more than 5,000 findings all exit non-zero with no output. With no `codes.json` it logs `not configured` and succeeds with no findings, so deleting `codes.json` closes its open issues as \"No longer detected\". ard does not run it on itself until ard has error codes.\n222:**The docs scanner** (`scanners/docs`, a `sh` script plus one stdlib-only Go program, `docscan.go`, built at scan time with the job's own Go) lists undocumented exported Go identifiers. It reports, per package directory: a missing package comment; an exported function, a method on an exported receiver type (pointer, value or generic) and an exported type with no doc comment (the spec's doc, its trailing comment or the enclosing declaration's doc all count); and an exported const or var with no comment, one finding per parenthesised block that has no block comment. Struct fields, interface methods, methods on unexported types and comment wording are not checked. Every exported identifier counts, `internal/` included, and files for every platform count (build constraints are not evaluated). It skips `testdata/`, `vendor/`, `node_modules/` and dot or underscore directories, files whose names start with `_` or `.`, symlinks, `*_test.go`, `//go:build ignore` files, generated files and `package main`. The fingerprint is `docs:undoc:<dir>:<symbol>` or `docs:pkg:<dir>`; the file and line are left out, so moving a declaration within its package keeps its issue. Severity is `low` and the group is the package directory. It only parses files: it never runs repository code and needs no network. It fails closed: a parse error, unreadable file, invalid path or more than 10,000 findings fails the run with nothing filed. A human-owned `exclude` file in the role directory skips package directories (`dir/` is a directory and everything below it, anything else a glob matched against the package directory); an entry makes its findings vanish, so autoclose closes their issues. Go only for now.\n418:  scanners/cve:             { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\n419:  scanners/docs:            { every: 3d, max_open: 3, group_size: 10, autoclose: true }","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2a8bf32-d6e4-436f-a631-72cb4dc75f8f","uuid":"d2bcc7af-732a-4038-8204-b92a0c59ba6a","timestamp":"2026-11-23T21:17:35.123Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, 